## How AI Works in Multiplayer

### Agent Session Model

In collaborative environments, each participant typically has an **independent agent session**:

| Participant | Agent Session | Context |
|------------|--------------|---------|
| **Host** | Independent | Own files, own Composer |
| **Participant 1** | Independent | Own files, own Composer |
| **Participant 2** | Independent | Own files, own Composer |

### Shared vs Independent Context

| Context Type | Shared? | Example |
|-------------|---------|---------|
| **File edits** | Yes (Live Share syncs files) | Both see changes in real-time |
| **Agent prompts** | No | Each person's Agent sees their own prompts |
| **Composer sessions** | No | Independent per participant |
| **Terminal output** | Shared (if terminal is shared) | Both see terminal output |
| **Agent Output channel** | No | Independent per participant |
| **Rules/Skills** | Yes (loaded from shared repo) | Both use same rules |

### AI Role in Pair Programming

| Scenario | AI Role |
|----------|---------|
| **Driver writes code** | AI assists the driver |
| **Navigator reviews** | AI can review navigator's questions |
| **Debugging together** | AI helps diagnose from both perspectives |
| **Code review** | AI provides independent review to both |
| **Architecture discussion** | AI provides options to both participants |

